WEBVTT

00:00:01.400 --> 00:00:04.000
Have you ever tried joining
your seams on the machine?

00:00:05.000 --> 00:00:08.400
This is a really fast
way to finish a garment.

00:00:08.760 --> 00:00:10.400
You'll be pleased with the results.

00:00:11.360 --> 00:00:16.720
First, hang the side of one piece on your
needles with the public or

00:00:16.800 --> 00:00:18.320
the right side facing you.

00:00:19.800 --> 00:00:25.320
Insert your 2 or 3-prong tool
and pick up the whole edge stitch across

00:00:25.480 --> 00:00:27.160
the piece and hang them on the needles.

00:00:29.520 --> 00:00:34.880
Next, hang the second piece on the same
needles with the private side facing you.

00:00:35.840 --> 00:00:39.440
Be sure that the public sides of the work
are sandwiched together

00:00:39.520 --> 00:00:41.080
and they're facing each other.

00:00:42.360 --> 00:00:47.240
The trick to a perfect seam is to pick
up both strands of the edge stitch only.

00:00:48.360 --> 00:00:52.280
When hanging the second piece,
you might sometimes pick up a leg or

00:00:52.400 --> 00:00:56.320
partial strand of an adjacent stitch,
ruining the entire

00:00:56.400 --> 00:00:57.600
appearance of your seam.

00:00:58.280 --> 00:01:02.840
So go slowly and make sure
the seam is perfect the first time.

00:01:06.960 --> 00:01:11.400
When both pieces are on the needles,
pull all of the needles all the way out

00:01:11.880 --> 00:01:13.160
and close the latches.

00:01:13.640 --> 00:01:15.000
Now don't skip this step.

00:01:15.080 --> 00:01:16.920
Make sure those latches are closed.

00:01:17.920 --> 00:01:23.333
Starting with the carriage on the left
with the main color yarn, knit 1 row.

00:01:23.920 --> 00:01:27.360
From left to right at the
tightest tension possible.

00:01:33.240 --> 00:01:37.520
Next, you'll manually knit a very
large stitch on each needle.

00:01:38.480 --> 00:01:41.840
Keep these stitches loose so
the seam has some stretch.

00:01:43.240 --> 00:01:46.720
You'll want these stitches
to be large but not sloppy.

00:01:47.760 --> 00:01:49.360
Practice until you get the tension

00:01:54.160 --> 00:01:56.133
At this point, your yarn

00:01:56.200 --> 00:01:57.400
will be on the left.

00:01:57.600 --> 00:02:01.040
So break the yarn with a—
leave about a 6-inch tail.

00:02:02.720 --> 00:02:06.800
Working from the right,
bind off the stitches on the needles

00:02:07.000 --> 00:02:10.520
by pulling each stitch through
its neighbor to the left.

00:02:12.480 --> 00:02:15.560
Finish by pulling the tail
through the last stitch.

00:02:19.360 --> 00:02:23.480
In this image, I used a contrasting
color to show you the seam.

00:02:24.560 --> 00:02:28.880
This method adds a bit of bulk,
but the bulk can be reduced by using

00:02:29.000 --> 00:02:30.720
a thinner yarn for the seam.

00:02:32.520 --> 00:02:37.200
Even with a highly contrasting color,
notice that this seam is very

00:02:37.360 --> 00:02:39.880
inconspicuous from the public side.

